{
 "id": "2025-krupinski-facade",
 "name": "Facade Floodlighting: Luminance Contrast Variants",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the online survey stage of Krupiński, Rusnak, Żagan, Kuczyński, Koszewicz, Szmigiel & Geniusz (2025), \"Composition and Contrast: The Painterly Nature of Architectural Exterior Illumination\", Arts 14(6):139. Three computer simulations of the floodlit façade of a neoclassical government building complex differ only in where luminance contrast falls, at the same average façade luminance of 10 cd/m²: A, the distant central part brighter and the closer wings darker; B, the closer wings brighter; C, even luminance. Adult participants who are in a dark room give their gender, age, profession, vision and screen type, then see the three variants together and choose which they like most and least and which gives the strongest sense of calm (one answer each), and which best conveys the building's official character, makes it look wider and creates the strongest spatial depth (several answers allowed). In the paper (N = 358 online; 68% liked A most, 63% liked B least, B gave the strongest depth) the survey complemented an eye-tracking study. Stimuli: the three simulations cropped from Figure 2 (a, c, e) of the paper (CC BY 4.0); questions and answer options from Table 1. The template will be removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the original also showed two filler variants (D and E, chosen as most liked by 18%), which are not printed in the paper, so only A, B and C are offered; (2) the wording of the background questions and their answer options is reconstructed from the paper's description; (3) the eye-tracking stage is not included; (4) the page asks for a dark room but cannot check it.",
